Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) can be either giants or dwarfs depending on distance; less directly indicative alone.  
- Plx (parallax): Smaller parallax means greater distance; giants tend to be more luminous and can be seen at larger distances with moderate brightness.  
- e_Plx (parallax error): High error reduces reliability of distance and absolute magnitude; less directly related to giant classification.  
- B-V (color index): Giants often have redder colors (higher B-V), but depends on spectral type; color can indicate temperature and evolutionary stage.  
- SpType (spectral type): The luminosity class in spectral type (e.g., III, II/III, III+) is a strong indicator of giant stars; V indicates main sequence (dwarf).  
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants have lower absolute magnitude values (brighter intrinsically) compared to dwarfs; however, the examples show some high values, possibly due to calculation or scale specifics.

