Answer:

Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) tend to be either closer or intrinsically brighter. Giants are intrinsically bright but can appear faint if far away. Alone, Vmag is not a strong indicator of giant status without distance.
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means closer star. Giants are luminous and can be seen at larger distances (smaller Plx). Smaller Plx may indicate distant giants.
- e_Plx (parallax error): High error reduces reliability of distance measurement; less useful directly for classification but can indicate uncertainty.
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature and spectral class. Giants tend to have characteristic B-V values depending on spectral type; e.g., giants are often redder (higher B-V) than dwarfs of same spectral type.
- SpType (spectral type): The luminosity class is embedded here (e.g., V = dwarf/main sequence, III = giant, II = bright giant). This is a strong indicator of whether the star is a giant.
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants have lower (brighter) absolute magnitudes than dwarfs of same spectral type. Higher Amag (fainter) indicates dwarf stars; lower Amag (brighter) indicates giants.

Step 2. Based on the above and examples, infer conditions.

10 different conditions for class "0" (not giant):

- SpType is in [G1V, F3V, G6/G8V, B5V, F0V, G2V-VI, F7V, G3V, F5V, K0V]
- Amag >= 18
- B-V <= 0.8
- Plx >= 10
- Vmag >= 7.5
- e_Plx <= 1.5
- Amag is within range [18, 22]
- Plx is within range [10, 22]
- B-V is within range [0.4, 0.8]
- SpType contains 'V' but not 'III' or 'II' (i.e., main sequence stars)

10 different conditions for class "1" (giant):

- SpType is in [K3II/III, K2III, B8II/III, B3III, G8III, K1/K2III+, G3/G5II/III, K2III, K2III]
- Amag < 17
- B-V > 1.1
- Plx < 5
- Vmag < 9
- e_Plx <= 1.5
- Amag is within range [11, 17]
- Plx is within range [0.5, 5]
- B-V is within range [1.1, 1.4]
- SpType contains 'III' or 'II' (luminosity class giant or bright giant)

These conditions align with the examples and astrophysical knowledge: giants have spectral types with luminosity class III or II, brighter absolute magnitudes (lower Amag), redder color indices (higher B-V), and tend to have smaller parallaxes (farther away). Dwarfs have luminosity class V, fainter absolute magnitudes (higher Amag), bluer colors (lower B-V), and larger parallaxes (closer).

