Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:  
- Vmag (apparent magnitude) depends on distance and intrinsic brightness; alone it does not distinguish giants well because giants can be bright but far away.  
- Plx (parallax) indicates distance; smaller parallax means farther star. Giants are luminous and can be seen at larger distances with small parallaxes.  
- e_Plx (error in parallax) affects reliability of distance estimate but does not directly indicate giant status.  
- B-V (color index) indicates star temperature and spectral class; giants tend to have certain B-V ranges but overlap with dwarfs exists.  
- SpType (spectral type) contains luminosity class (V = dwarf, III = giant, II = bright giant, IV = subgiant); this is a very strong indicator of giant status.  
- Amag (absolute magnitude) indicates intrinsic brightness; giants have lower (brighter) absolute magnitudes compared to dwarfs of similar spectral type.  

Step 2. Based on Step 1 and examples:  

10 different conditions for class "0" (not giant):  
- SpType is in [types containing "V" only, e.g., G6/G8V, K0V, G3V, G2V-VI, F7V, B5V, F5V, F0V]  
- Amag (absolute magnitude) >= 15.5  
- B-V is within range [0.2, 0.8] (typical for dwarfs in sample)  
- Plx >= 5 (closer stars tend to be dwarfs here)  
- Vmag >= 6.5 and Vmag <= 10 (apparent magnitude range for dwarfs shown)  
- e_Plx <= 1.5 (measurement error not too large for reliable classification)  
- SpType does not contain "III" or "II" or "IV"  
- Amag >= 15 and Plx >= 5 simultaneously  
- B-V < 1.0 (dwarfs tend to have smaller B-V in examples)  
- SpType is in [G1V, F3V, K5V, F7V, B5V, F5V, F0V] (common dwarf spectral types)  

10 different conditions for class "1" (giant):  
- SpType is in [types containing "III", "II", "III/IV", "II/III", "III+"] (e.g., K2III, G8III, B3III, B8II/III, G3/G5II/III, K1/K2III+, K3II/III)  
- Amag < 16 (giants are intrinsically brighter, so smaller absolute magnitude)  
- B-V >= 1.0 (giants tend to be redder)  
- Plx <= 3 (giants tend to be farther away)  
- Vmag between 7 and 9.5 (observed apparent magnitude range for giants in examples)  
- SpType contains luminosity class "III" or "II" or "IV"  
- Amag is within range [11, 16]  
- B-V > 1.1 (red giants in examples)  
- Plx < 5 and Amag < 16 simultaneously  
- SpType is in [K2III, G8III, B3III, B8II/III, G3/G5II/III, K1/K2III+, K3II/III]  

These conditions align well with the examples and astrophysical knowledge of giant vs dwarf stars.

